  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/pterodoras-granulosus-4/",
      "html": "<p>The Thorny Catfishes (Doradidae) are a small family of exclusively South American catfish. Currently 41 genera containing 127 species are known, but new species continue to be discovered. The largest species attain a maximum length of more than 100 cm. The world record for Opsodoras niger is 120 cm and a weight of 20 kg. The smallest species, Physopyxis lyra, becomes only 3.5 cm long. Most species attain a maximum length between 10 and 20 cm.</p><p>Pterodoras granulosus from Peru is a large species, which can become around 70 cm long. Juveniles of 7-10 cm were obtained.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/doradidae/pterodoras_granulosus.htm",
      "html": "<ul><li>Common name: Granulated catfish</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/doradidae/pterodoras_granulosus.htm",
      "html": "<p>South America: Amazon and Paraná River basins and coastal drainages in Guyana and Surinam.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/doradidae/pterodoras_granulosus.htm",
      "html": "<p>In their natural habitat they would form small groups and feed mainly on molluscs and the fruits of Astrocaryum javary.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/doradidae/pterodoras_granulosus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Would need a large tank with large pipes, plastic or ceramic, to rest in. House with same size or larger tank mates.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/doradidae/pterodoras_granulosus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Pellets, tablets, frozen mysis shrimp, worm food and mussels. You could also try various fruits such as bananas.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/pterodoras-granulosus-4/",
      "html": "<p>This species is as peaceful as any species of thorny catfish, but one should keep in mind that the fish has a large mouth and smaller fish will vanish there of course.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/doradidae/pterodoras_granulosus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: The specific name granulosus: granum (L.), seed or grain; –osus (L.), suffix connoting fullness, presumably referring to 23–28 shallow lateral plates (or scutes) along length of body, which may give the scaleless skin a granulated appearance.</p><p>IUCN Red List: Despite the little information available on this species, it has a wide range and no apparent threats. Therefore, it is assessed as Least Concern (IUCN 2023).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/doradidae/pterodoras_granulosus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Salvador, G.N. 2023. Pterodoras granulosus. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2023. ScotCat Factsheet no. 238. April 2016. The ETYFish Project Fish Name Etymology Database © Christopher Scharpf.</p>"
    }
  ],
